# Assembly theory, the measure of how much history an object carries. The assembly index of an object is the least number
# of joining steps needed to build it from basic parts, where any sub-assembly already made can be reused for free. An
# object with a low assembly index can be reached by a short shared path; a high index means the object is complex,
# requiring many distinct steps that could not have arisen by chance without a process that remembers its intermediates.
# The clearest case is doubling: a run of one part becomes a run of two in one step, four in two steps, eight in three, so
# a run of two to the k parts has assembly index k, the logarithm, because each step reuses the whole run built so far. A
# chain with no repetition has no reuse, so its naive cost is one less than its length, every part joined separately. The
# gap between the two, the logarithm below the length, is the compression that reuse buys, and it is why assembly index
# distinguishes a structured object from a random one of the same size. This file checks the doubling index, the
# no-reuse chain cost, and that reuse strictly beats the naive path.

# A run of eight identical parts is built by doubling: one to two to four to eight, three steps, so the assembly index of
# 2^3 = 8 is 3.
hold assembly-index-of-eight-is-three
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 2

# A run of sixteen parts doubles in four steps, assembly index 4: 16 = 2^4.
hold assembly-index-of-sixteen-is-four
  call is-equal
    code 16
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        call multiply
          code 2
          code 2

# A chain of eight distinct parts with no reuse costs one join per new part after the first, 8 - 1 = 7 steps.
hold no-reuse-chain-of-eight-costs-seven
  call is-equal
    code 7
    call subtract
      code 8
      code 1

# Reuse strictly beats the naive path: the doubling index three is below the no-reuse cost seven for the same length
# eight, so a structured object of a given size is cheaper to assemble than a random one, the compression assembly theory
# measures.
hold reuse-beats-the-naive-path
  call is-below
    code 3
    code 7
